== Chapter IV

#align(center)[#block(width: 85%)[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #strong[Showing that the worst things work for good to the godly.]
]]

#v(0.9em)

2\. #smallcaps[The] #emph[worst things] work for good to the godly #footnote[καὶ πάντα περιτρέπει ὁ θεὸς εἰς ἀγαθά. \[And God turns all things to good.\] #emph[Aecumon.]]. Do not mistake me. I do not say that they are good in their own nature #footnote[Evil things in their own nature do not work together, but work against.], for they are a fruit of the curse. But though they are #emph[naturally] evil, yet they are #emph[morally] good, as the wise, overruling hand of God disposes and sanctifies them. Take the elements: though they are of contrary qualities, yet God has so tempered them that they all work in a harmonious way for the good of the universe. Or take a watch: the wheels seem to move contrary to one another, but all carry on the motion of the watch and help to make the alarm strike. So things that seem to move across the path of the godly yet work for their good by the wonderful providence of God. Among these κάκιστα, worst things, there are four sad evils that work for good to those who love God.

=== SECTION I. Showing that the evil of affliction works for good to the godly.

1\. #smallcaps[The] evil of affliction works for good. There are two heart-quieting considerations in all the afflictions that befall us.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. The first is that God has a special hand in them: “The Almighty has afflicted me” (Ruth 1:21). Instruments can no more stir until God gives them a commission than the axe can cut by itself without a hand. #emph[Job] saw God in his affliction. So, as #emph[Augustine] observes, he does not say, “The LORD gave, and the devil took away” #footnote[#emph[Non dixit] Job, #emph[Deus dedit, Diabolus abstulit.]], but, “The LORD has taken away.” Whoever brings an affliction to us, it is God who sends it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. The second heart-quieting consideration is that afflictions work for good. Jer 24:5: “Like these good figs, so I regard as good the exiles from Judah, whom I have sent away from this place to the land of the Chaldeans for their good.” #emph[Judah’s] captivity in #emph[Babylon] was לטובה, #emph[for their good.] Ps 119:71: “It was good for me to be afflicted” #footnote[χάριν ὁμολογεῖ τοῖς αὐστηροτέροις τοῦ ἰατροῦ φαρμάκοις. \[He gives thanks for the physician’s harsher medicines.\] #emph[Theod.]]. This text, like the tree #emph[Moses] threw into the bitter waters, may make the bitter waters of affliction sweet and wholesome to drink. Afflictions to the godly are medicinal. Out of the most poisonous drug God draws our salvation. Afflictions are as needful as ordinances (1 Pet 1:6). No vessel can be made of gold without fire; so it is impossible that we should be made vessels of honor unless we are melted and refined in the furnace of affliction. Ps 25:10: “All the LORD’s paths are mercy”: his bloody paths are mercy. As the painter mixes bright colors with dark shadows, so the wise God mixes #emph[mercy] with #emph[judgment.] The afflicting providences that seem to be harmful are beneficial. Let us take some instances in Scripture. #emph[Joseph’s] brothers threw him into a pit, afterward they sold him, then he was cast into prison; yet all this worked for his good. His abasement made way for his advancement: he was made the second man in the kingdom. Gen 50:20: “As for you#super[+], what you#super[+] intended against me for evil, God intended for good.” #emph[Jacob] wrestled with the angel, and the hollow of #emph[Jacob’s] thigh was put out of joint. This was sad, but God turned it to good, for there he saw God’s face, and there the Lord blessed him (Gen 32:30): “So Jacob named the place Peniel, saying, ‘Indeed, I have seen God face to face.’” Who would not be willing to have a bone out of joint, if he might have a sight of God? King #emph[Manasseh] was bound in chains. It was sad to see a crown of gold changed into fetters, but it worked for his good, for “in his affliction, Manasseh sought the favor of the LORD his God and earnestly humbled himself … and the LORD received his plea” (2 Chr 33:11, 12). He owed more to his iron chain than to his gold chain: the one made him proud, the other made him humble. #emph[Job] was a spectacle of misery. He lost all that he ever had; he abounded only in boils and ulcers. This was sad, but it worked for his good: his grace was more proved and improved. God gave a testimony from heaven to his integrity, and made up his loss by giving him twice as much as he ever had before (Job 42:10). #emph[Paul] was struck with blindness. This was distressing, but it turned to his good. By that blindness God made way for the light of grace to shine into his soul; it was the beginning of a happy conversion (Acts 9:6).]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[As the hard frosts in winter bring on the flowers in the spring, and as the night ushers in the morning star, so the evils of affliction produce much good to those who love God. But we are ready to question the truth of this, and to say as #emph[Mary] did to the angel, “How can this be?” So I will show you#super[+] several ways in which affliction works for good.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. Affliction works for good as it is our preacher and tutor: “Heed the rod” (Mic 6:9) #footnote[#emph[Cum vibices dorso meo imprimeres, praecepta tua cordi meo insculpsisti.] \[When You printed stripes on my back, You engraved Your precepts on my heart.\] Rivet. in Psal.]. #emph[Luther] says that he could never rightly understand some of the #emph[Psalms] until he was in affliction. Affliction teaches two things. 1. What sin is. In the word preached we hear what a dreadful thing sin is, that it both defiles and damns; but we fear it no more than a painted lion. So God lets affliction loose, and then we feel how bitter sin is in its fruit. A sickbed often teaches more than a sermon; we can best see the ugly face of sin in the mirror of affliction. 2. Affliction teaches us to know ourselves. In prosperity we are for the most part strangers to ourselves. God makes us know affliction so that we may know ourselves better. In a time of affliction we see corruption in our hearts that we would not believe was there. Water in the glass looks clear, but set it on the fire, and the scum boils up. In prosperity a man seems to be humble and thankful, and the water looks clear; but set this man on the fire of affliction a little, and the scum boils up: much impatience and unbelief appear. “Oh,” says a Christian, “I never thought I had such a bad heart as I now see I have. I never thought my corruptions were so strong and my graces so weak.”]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. Afflictions work for good as they are a means to make the heart more upright. In prosperity the heart is apt to be divided (Hos 10:2). The heart clings partly to God and partly to the world. It is like a needle between two lodestones: God draws, and the world draws. Now God takes away the world, so that the heart may cling more to him in sincerity. #emph[Corrigere, quasi cor rectum facere]\; correction is setting the heart right and straight. As we sometimes hold a crooked stick over the fire to straighten it, so God holds us over the fire of affliction to make us more straight and upright. Oh how good it is, when sin has bent the soul away from God, that affliction should straighten it again!]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. Afflictions work for good as they conform us to Christ. God’s rod is a pencil to draw Christ’s image on us more vividly. It is good that there should be a symmetry and proportion between the Head and the members. Would we be parts of Christ’s mystical body, and not be like him? His life (as #emph[Calvin] says) was a series of sufferings: “a man of sorrows, acquainted with grief” (Isa 53:3). He wept; he bled. Was the Head crowned with thorns, and do we think to be crowned with roses? It is good to be like Christ, though it is by sufferings. Jesus Christ drank a bitter cup; it made him sweat drops of blood to think of it. And though it is true that he drank the poison in the cup (#emph[the wrath of God]), yet there is some wormwood left in the cup, which the saints must drink. Only here is the difference between Christ’s sufferings and ours: his were to make satisfaction for sin; ours are only to correct.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. Afflictions work for good to the godly as they are destructive to sin. Sin is the mother, affliction is the daughter; the daughter helps to destroy the mother. Sin is like the tree that breeds the worm, and affliction is like the worm that eats the tree. There is much corruption in the best heart, and affliction works it out by degrees, as the fire works the dross out of the gold. Isa 27:9: “the full fruit of the removal of his sin.” What if we have more of the rough file, if we have less rust? Afflictions carry away nothing but the filth of sin. Suppose a physician should say to a patient, “Your body is diseased and full of bad humors, which must be purged out, or you die; but I will prescribe medicine which, though it makes you deadly sick, yet will carry away the dregs of your disease and save your life.” Would this not be for the good of the patient? Afflictions are the purging pills God uses to carry away our spiritual diseases. They cure the swelling of pride, the fever of lust, the dropsy of covetousness. Do they not then work for good?]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[5\. Afflictions work for good as they are a means to loosen our hearts from the world. When you dig the earth away from the root of a tree, it is to loosen the tree from the earth. So God digs away our earthly comforts to loosen our hearts from the earth #footnote[#emph[Eveniunt mala in hac vita, ne viator tendens ad patriam stabulum pro domo diligat.] \[Evils come in this life, so that the traveler on his way to his homeland may not love the inn instead of his home.\] Augustine]. We read of a star (Rev 8:11): “The name of the star is Wormwood.” Have we not seen this star appear? Do we not find this #emph[star Wormwood] in every condition? A thorn grows up with every flower.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Surgit amari aliquid quod in ipsis floribus angat.] \[Something bitter rises up to pain us among the very flowers.\]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[God would have the world hang like a loose tooth, which does not trouble us much when it is pulled away. Is it not good to be weaned? The oldest saint needs it. Why does the Lord break the conduit pipe, but that we may go to him in whom are #emph[all our fresh springs] #footnote[Ps 87:7.]?]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[6\. Afflictions work for good as they make way for comfort. God makes “the Valley of Achor into a gateway of hope” (Hos 2:15). #emph[Achor] means trouble. God sweetens outward pain with inward peace. John 16:20: “Your#super[+] grief will turn to joy.” Here is the water turned into wine. After a bitter pill God gives sugar. #emph[Paul] had his prison songs. God’s rod has honey at the end of it. The saints in affliction have had such sweet raptures of joy that they have thought themselves on the borders of the heavenly #emph[Canaan]\; they have gathered #emph[grapes from thorns.]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[7\. Afflictions work for good as they magnify us. Job 7:17: “What is man that You should magnify him … that You attend to him every morning?” God magnifies us by affliction in three ways. 1. In that he will stoop so low as to take notice of us, and will afflict us rather than lose us. It is an honor that God will mind dust and ashes; it magnifies us that God thinks us worthy to be struck. God’s not striking is a slighting: “Why should you#super[+] be stricken any more?” (Isa 1:5). If you#super[+] will go on in sin, take your#super[+] course, sin yourselves into hell. 2. Afflictions magnify us as they are #emph[insignia honoris,] ensigns of glory, signs of sonship. Heb 12:7: “Endure suffering as discipline; God is treating you#super[+] as sons.” Every print of the rod is a badge of honor. 3. Afflictions really tend to magnify the saints, as they make them renowned in the world. Soldiers have never been so admired for their victories as the saints have been for their sufferings. The zeal and constancy of the martyrs in their trials have made them famous to later ages. How eminent #emph[Job] was for his patience! God leaves his name on record: “You#super[+] have heard of Job’s patience” (Jas 5:11). #emph[Job] the sufferer was more renowned than #emph[Alexander] the conqueror.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[8\. Afflictions work for good as they are a means to make us happy. Job 5:17: “Happy is the man whom God corrects.” What politician or moralist ever placed happiness in the cross? #emph[Job] does: “Happy is the man whom God corrects.”]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Question.] How do afflictions make us happy?]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Answer.] In that afflictions, when they are sanctified, bring us nearer to God #footnote[συλλέγει πρὸς θεὸν. \[It gathers us to God.\] #emph[Cyril.]]. The moon at the full is furthest from the sun; so many are furthest from God in the full moon of prosperity, and affliction brings them nearer to God. The lodestone of mercy does not draw us so near to God as the cords of affliction do. When #emph[Absalom] set #emph[Joab’s] grain on fire, Joab came running to #emph[Absalom] (2 Sam 14:30). When God sets our worldly comforts on fire, then we run to him and make our peace with him. When the #emph[prodigal] was pinched with want, then he returned home to his father (Luke 15:18). When the dove could find no rest for the sole of her foot, she flew to the ark; when God brings a flood of affliction on us, then we fly to the ark, Christ. So affliction makes us happy by bringing us nearer to God #footnote[#emph[Mala quae hic nos premunt ad Deum ire cogunt.] \[The evils that press on us here compel us to go to God.\]]. Faith can make use of the waters of affliction to swim faster to Christ.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[9\. Afflictions work for good as they put the wicked to silence. How ready they are to slander and malign the godly, saying that they serve God only for self-interest! So God will have his people endure sufferings for religion, that he may hang a padlock on the lying lips of wicked men. When the atheists of the world see that God has a people who serve him not for wages but for love, this stops their mouths. The devil accused #emph[Job] of hypocrisy, of being a mercenary man whose religion was all made up of aims at gold and silver. Job 1:9, 10: “Does Job fear God for nothing? Have You not placed a hedge … around him?” and so on. “Well,” says God, “put out your hand; touch his estate.” No sooner had the devil received a commission than he fell to breaking down #emph[Job’s] hedge. Yes, but #emph[Job] still worships God (chapter 1:20), and professes his faith in him (chapter 13:15): “Though He slay me, I will trust in Him.” This amazed and silenced the devil himself. How it strikes a chill into wicked men when they see that the godly will #emph[iratum colere numen,] keep close to God in a suffering condition, and when they lose all, will still hold fast their integrity!]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[10\. Afflictions work for good as they make way for glory (2 Cor 4:17). Not that they #emph[merit] glory, but they #emph[prepare] for it. As plowing prepares the earth for a crop, so afflictions prepare us and make us fit for glory. The painter lays his gold on dark colors. So God first lays on the dark colors of affliction, and then he lays on the golden color of glory. The vessel is first seasoned before wine is poured into it; the vessels of mercy are first seasoned with affliction, and then the wine of glory is poured in #footnote[#emph[Ad tempus affligi bonum hominem etiam ex benignitate est, ut scilicet copiosius donetur, & beatior tanta fiat.] \[That a good man is afflicted for a time also comes from kindness: namely, so that he may be more richly rewarded and made so much the happier.\] Lud. Vives.]. So we see that afflictions are not harmful but beneficial to the saints. We should not look so much at the evil of affliction as at the good, not so much at the #emph[dark side] of the cloud as at the #emph[light.] The worst that God does to his children is to whip them to heaven.]]

=== SECTION II. Showing that the evil of temptation works for good to the godly.

2\. #smallcaps[The] evil of temptation works for good. Satan is called ὁ πειράζων, the #emph[tempter] (Matt 4:3). He is always lying in ambush: #emph[stat in procinctu Diabolus] \[the devil stands ready for battle\]. He is continually at work with one saint or another #footnote[ὁ διάβολος ἐπιθηρᾶται πλέον, ὅταν ἴδῃ τὰ φορτία τῆς ἀρετῆς συγκείμενα, καὶ πολὺν τὸν ὄγκον. \[The devil hunts the harder when he sees the loads of virtue piled together, and their great bulk.\] #emph[Chrysost.]]. The devil has his circuit, or diocese, that he walks every day. He is not yet fully cast into prison, but like a prisoner who is out on bail, he #emph[walks about] to tempt the saints. This is a great trouble to a child of God, as it is a trouble to a virgin to have her chastity assaulted daily. Now concerning Satan’s temptations, there are three things to be considered:

#[
#set enum(indent: 24pt)
+ His method in tempting.
+ The extent of his power.
+ That these temptations work for good.
]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. Satan’s method in tempting. Here take notice of two things.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. His #emph[violence] in tempting, and so he is the #emph[red dragon.] He labors to storm the castle of the heart. He throws in thoughts of blasphemy; he tempts us to deny God. These are the #emph[fiery darts] he shoots, by which he would inflame the passions.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. His #emph[subtlety] in tempting, and so he is the #emph[old serpent.] There are five chief subtleties the devil uses.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. He observes a man’s temper and constitution, and lays baits of temptation to suit it. As the farmer knows what grain is right for the soil, so Satan will not tempt contrary to the natural disposition and temperament. This is his policy: he makes the wind and the tide go together. The way the natural tide of the heart runs, that way the wind of temptation blows. Though the devil cannot know men’s #emph[thoughts,] yet he knows their #emph[temper,] and he lays his baits accordingly. —#emph[Omnium discutit more] \[he examines the ways of all\]— He tempts the ambitious man with a crown, the sanguine man with beauty.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. Satan watches for the fittest time to tempt, as a cunning angler casts in his hook when the fish will bite best. Satan’s time of tempting is usually after an ordinance, and the reason is that #emph[then] he thinks he will find us most off our guard. When we have been at solemn duties, we are apt to think all is done, and we grow slack and leave off the zeal and strictness we had before. We are just like a soldier who takes off his armor after a battle, never once dreaming of an enemy. Now Satan watches his time, and when we least suspect it, he throws in a temptation.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. He makes use of near relations; the devil tempts by proxy. So he handed a temptation to #emph[Job] by his wife: “Do you still retain your integrity?” (Job 2:9). A wife in the bosom may be the devil’s instrument to tempt to sin.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. Satan tempts to evil by those who are good; so he gives poison in a golden cup. He tempted Christ by #emph[Peter.] #emph[Peter] tried to dissuade him from suffering: “Master, pity yourself.” Who would have thought to find the tempter in the mouth of an apostle?]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[5\. Satan tempts to sin under a pretense of religion. He is most to be feared when he “masquerades as an angel of light.” He came to Christ with Scripture in his mouth: “It is written.” The devil baits his hook with religion. He tempts many a man to covetousness and extortion under a pretense of providing for his family. He tempts some to do away with themselves, so that they may live no longer to sin against God; and so he draws them into sin under a pretense of avoiding sin. These are his νοήματα, his subtle stratagems in tempting.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. The extent of his power: how far Satan’s power in tempting reaches.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. He can set the object before us, as he set a bar of gold before #emph[Achan.]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. He can poison the imagination and instill evil thoughts into the mind. As the Holy Spirit casts in good motions, so the devil casts in bad ones. He put it into #emph[Judas’s] heart to betray Christ (John 13:2).—]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. Satan can stir up and provoke the corruption within, and work a kind of inclination in the heart to embrace a temptation. It is true that Satan cannot force the will to consent; yet, being an earnest suitor, he may provoke to evil by his continual soliciting. So he provoked #emph[David] to number the people (1 Chr 21:1).—The devil may argue us into sin by his subtle arguments.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. These temptations work for good to the children of God. A tree that is shaken by the wind is more settled and rooted; so the blowing of a temptation only settles a Christian the more in grace. Temptations work for good in eight ways.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. Temptation sends the soul to prayer. The more furiously Satan tempts, the more fervently the saint prays. The deer, when it is shot with the dart, runs faster to the water; when Satan shoots his fiery darts at the soul, it runs faster to the throne of grace. When #emph[Paul] had the messenger of Satan to buffet him: “Three times I pleaded with the Lord to take it away from me” (2 Cor 12:8). Temptation is a medicine for careless security. Whatever makes us pray more works for good.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. Temptation to sin is a means to keep us from committing sin. The more a child of God is tempted, the more he fights against the temptation. The more Satan tempts to blasphemy, the more a saint trembles at such thoughts and says, “Away from me, Satan!” When #emph[Joseph’s] mistress tempted him to folly, the stronger her temptation was, the stronger his resistance was. The temptation the devil uses as a spur to sin, God makes a bridle to hold a Christian back from it. The more a chaste virgin is assaulted, the more she abhors the proposal.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. Temptation works for good as it brings down the swelling of pride. 2 Cor 12:7: “So to keep me from becoming conceited, I was given a thorn in my flesh, a messenger of Satan, to torment me.” The thorn in the flesh was to prick the bladder of pride #footnote[οὐδὲν γὰρ ἡμῶν τῇ φύσει οὕτω συμβάλλεται ὡς τὸ φύσημα τῆς ψυχῆς καταστέλλεσθαι. \[For nothing does our nature so much good as having the swelling of the soul brought down.\] #emph[Chrysost.]]. Better is the temptation that humbles me than the duty that makes me proud. Rather than let a Christian be haughty-minded, God will let him fall into the devil’s hands for a while, to be cured of his abscess.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. Temptation works for good as it is a touchstone to try what is in the heart. The word #emph[tentare] \[to tempt\] means #emph[explorare] \[to search out\]. The devil tempts so that he may deceive; but God allows us to be tempted to try us. 1. Temptation is a trial of our sincerity. It shows that our heart is chaste and loyal to Christ when we can look a temptation in the face and turn our back on it. 2. It is a trial of our courage. Hos 7:11: “Ephraim has become like a silly dove, without a heart.” So it may be said of many that #emph[they are without a heart]: they have no heart to resist temptation. No sooner does Satan come than they yield, like a coward who gives the thief his purse as soon as he approaches. But he is the valiant Christian who brandishes the sword of the Spirit against Satan, and will rather die than yield. The courage of the #emph[Romans] was never more seen than when they were assaulted by the #emph[Carthaginians]\; the valor and might of a saint is never more seen than in a pitched battle, when he is fighting with the #emph[red dragon] and by the power of faith puts the devil to flight. That grace is #emph[tried gold] which can stand in the #emph[fiery trial] and withstand #emph[fiery darts.] #emph[Fidei robur potest esse concussum, non excussum] \[The strength of faith may be shaken, but not shaken off\] #footnote[#emph[Tertul.]].]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[5\. Temptations work for good as God makes those who are tempted fit to comfort others in the same distress. A Christian must himself be under the buffetings of Satan before he can speak a word #emph[in due season] to him who is weary. St. #emph[Paul] was well versed in temptations: “We are not unaware of his schemes” (2 Cor 2:11). And he was able to acquaint others with Satan’s cursed wiles (1 Cor 10:13). A man who has ridden over a place where there are bogs and quicksands is the fittest to guide others through that dangerous way. He who has felt the claws of the roaring lion, and has lain bleeding under those wounds, is the fittest man to deal with one who is tempted. None can better uncover Satan’s tricks and policies than those who have been long in the fencing school of temptation.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[6\. Temptations work for good as they stir up a father’s compassion in God toward those who are tempted. The child who is sick and bruised is looked after most. When a saint lies under the bruisings of temptation, Christ prays, and God the Father pities. When Satan puts the soul into a fever, God comes with a cordial. That is what made #emph[Luther] say that temptations are #emph[amplexus Christi, Christ’s embraces,] because he then most sweetly reveals himself to the soul.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[7\. Temptations work for good as they make the saints long more for heaven. There they will be out of gunshot. Heaven is a place of rest; no bullets of temptation fly there. The eagle that soars aloft in the air and sits on high trees is not troubled with the stinging of the serpent. So when believers have gotten up into the highest heaven, they will not be troubled by the old serpent #footnote[#emph[Nullae ibi insidiae Daemonum.] \[There are no snares of demons there.\] Bernard]. In this life, when one temptation is over, another comes. This is to make God’s people long for the time when death sounds a retreat and calls them off the field where the bullets fly so thick, to receive a victor’s crown, where not the drum or the cannon, but the harp and the viol, will always be sounding.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[8\. Temptations work for good as they engage the strength of Christ. Christ is our friend, and when we are tempted, he sets all his power to work for us #footnote[#emph[Venit Diabolus, subvenit Christus.] \[The devil comes; Christ comes to help.\]]: “Because He Himself suffered when He was tempted, He is able to help those who are being tempted” #footnote[#emph[Deficientem sublevat, et vincentem coronat.] \[He lifts up the one who is failing, and crowns the one who conquers.\] Augustine]. If a poor soul had to fight alone with the #emph[Goliath] of hell, he would be sure to be overcome. But Jesus Christ brings in his auxiliary forces; he gives fresh supplies of grace (2 Cor 12:9). And in him ὑπερνικῶμὲν, “we are more than conquerors.” So the evil of temptation works for good.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But sometimes Satan defeats a child of God; how does this work for good#footnote[#emph[Question.]]?]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I grant that through the suspension of divine grace and the fury of a temptation a saint may be overcome. Yet this defeat by a temptation will work for good#footnote[#emph[Answer.]]. 1. By this defeat God makes way for the increase of grace. #emph[Peter] was tempted to self-confidence; he presumed on his own strength. And when he would insist on standing alone, Christ let him fall. But this worked for his good. It cost him many a tear: “he went outside and wept bitterly.” And now he grew more modest. He dared not say that he loved Christ more than the other apostles. John 21:15: “Do you love Me more than these?” He dared not say so; his fall broke the neck of his pride. 2. Defeat by a temptation causes more circumspection and watchfulness in a child of God. Though Satan decoyed him into sin before, yet for the future he will be the more cautious. He will take care not to come within the lion’s chain any more. He is more shy and fearful of the #emph[occasions] of sin. He never goes out without his spiritual armor, and he girds on his armor by prayer. He knows he walks on slippery ground, so he looks warily to his steps. He keeps close sentry in his soul, and when he spies the devil coming, he stands to his arms and holds up #emph[the shield of faith] (Eph 6:16). This is all the hurt the devil does: when he defeats a saint by temptation, he cures him of his careless neglect, and makes him watch and pray more. When wild beasts get over the hedge and hurt the grain, a man will make his fence the stronger. So when the devil gets over the hedge by a temptation, a Christian will be sure to mend his fence; he is more fearful of sin, and more careful of duty. So being worsted by temptation works for good.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Objection.] But if being defeated works for good, may this not make Christians careless whether they are overcome by temptations or not?]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Response.] There is a great deal of difference between falling into a temptation and running into a temptation. Falling into a temptation will work for good, not running into it. He who falls into a river may be helped and pitied, but he who recklessly plunges into it is guilty of his own death. It is madness to run into a lion’s den. He who runs himself into a temptation is like #emph[Saul,] who “took his own sword and fell on it.”]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[From all that has been said, by way of conclusion, see how God makes a fool of the old serpent, by making his temptations turn to the good of his people. Surely if the devil knew how much benefit comes to the saints by temptation, he would stop tempting#footnote[#emph[Tentationes quas Diabolus commovet ad interitum, Deus convertit ad profectum.] \[The temptations the devil stirs up for our destruction, God turns to our progress.\] Bernard #emph[de Teni.]]. #emph[Luther] once said that three things make a Christian: #emph[prayer, meditation, temptation.] After a fever the body shoots up more in stature; after believers have been in a hot fit of temptation, they have shot up more in holiness. St. #emph[Paul] on his voyage to #emph[Rome] met with a contrary wind (Acts 27:4). So the wind of temptation is a wind contrary to that of the Spirit; but God makes use of this cross wind to blow the saints to heaven.]]

=== SECTION III. Showing that the evil of desertion works for good to the godly.

3\. #smallcaps[The] evil of #emph[desertion] works for good. The bride complains of desertion. Song 5:6: “my beloved had withdrawn himself and gone.” There is a twofold withdrawing. Either it is 1. in regard of grace, when God suspends the influence of his Spirit and withholds the lively actings of grace. If the Spirit is gone, grace freezes into chilliness and numbness. Or it is 2. a withdrawing in regard of comfort, when God withholds the sweet manifestations of his favor. He does not look with such a pleasant face, but veils his face, and seems to be quite gone from the soul.

God is just in all his withdrawings: we desert him before he deserts us #footnote[#emph[Tu me non deseris nisi prius ego te deseram.] \[You do not forsake me unless I first forsake You.\] Augustine.]. We desert God when we leave off close communion with him; when we desert his truths and dare not appear for him; when we leave the guidance and direction of his word and follow the #emph[ignis fatuus] \[will-o’-the-wisp\] of our own corrupt affections and passions. We usually desert God first, so we have no one to blame but ourselves.

Desertion is very sad. For as darkness follows in the air when the light is withdrawn, so when God withdraws there is darkness and sorrow in the soul. Desertion is #emph[an agony of conscience,] as #emph[Alstead] calls it; God holds the soul over hell. Job 6:4: “The arrows of the Almighty have pierced me; my spirit drinks in their poison.” It was a custom among the #emph[Persians] in their wars (as #emph[Drusius] notes) to dip their arrows in the poison of serpents to make them more deadly. So God shot the poisoned arrow of desertion into #emph[Job,] and under its wounds his spirit lay bleeding. In times of desertion the people of God are apt to be dejected. They dispute against themselves and think that God has quite cast them off. So I will prescribe some comfort to the deserted soul. When the sailor has no star to guide him, yet he has light in his lantern, which is some help to him. So when the poor soul is sailing in the dark of desertion and lacks the #emph[bright morning star,] I will lay down four consolations, which are like the sailor’s lantern to give some light.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. None but the godly are capable of desertion. Wicked men do not know what God’s love means, nor what it is to lack it. They know what it is to lack health, friends, and trade, but not what it is to lack God’s favor. You fear that you are not God’s child because you are deserted, whereas none but the godly are deserted. The Lord cannot be said to withdraw his love from the wicked, because they never had it. Being deserted shows you to be a child of God. How could you complain that God has estranged himself, if you had not at times received smiles and love tokens from him?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. There may be the seed of grace where there is not the flower of joy. The earth may lack a crop of grain, yet may have a mine of gold within. A Christian may have grace within, though the luscious fruit of joy does not grow. Ships at sea that are richly laden with jewels and spices may be in the dark and be tossed in the storm. A soul enriched with the treasures of grace may yet be in the dark of desertion, and so tossed as to think it will be cast away in the storm. #emph[David,] in a state of desolation, prays, “take not Your Holy Spirit from me” (Ps 51:11). He does not pray, says #emph[Augustine,] “Lord, give me your Spirit,” but, #emph[Ne tollas spiritum,] “Do not take away your Spirit.” So he still had the Spirit of God remaining in him.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. These desertions are only for a time. Christ may go into the withdrawing room and leave the soul for a while, but he will come again. Isa 54:8: “In a little wrath I hid My face from you for a moment, but with everlasting kindness I will have compassion on you.” When it is dead low water, the tide will come in again. Isa 57:16: “I will not always be angry; for then the spirit of man would grow weak before Me, with the breath of those I have made.” The tender mother sets her child down in anger, but she will take it up into her arms again and kiss it. God may put the soul away in anger, but he will take it up again into his dear embraces and display the banner of love over it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. These desertions work for good to the godly, and in seven ways.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. Desertion works a cure on the soul. 1. It cures sinful drowsiness. We find the bride fallen on the bed of sloth: “I sleep” (Song 5:2). And at once Christ was gone (verse 6): “my beloved had withdrawn himself.” Who will speak to someone who is drowsy? 2. Desertion cures excessive love of the world. 1 John 2:15: “Do not love the world.” We may hold the world like a posy in our hand, but it must not lie too near our heart. We may use it as an inn where we stop for a meal, but it must not be our home. Perhaps these worldly things steal the heart away too much. Good men are at times sick with a surfeit and drunk with the luscious delights of prosperity. They have spotted their silver wings of grace and much defaced God’s image by rubbing it against the earth. To recover them from this, the Lord hides his face in a cloud. This eclipse has good effects: it darkens all the glory of the world and makes it disappear.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. Desertion works for good as it makes the saints prize God’s countenance more than ever. Ps 63:3: “Your loving devotion is better than life.” Yet the commonness of this mercy lowers its value. When pearls grew common at #emph[Rome,] they began to be slighted. God has no better way to make us value his love than by withdrawing it for a while. If the sun shone only once a year, how it would be prized! When the soul has long been benighted with desertion, oh how welcome then is the return of the Sun of Righteousness!]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. Desertion works for good as it is a means to make sin bitter to us. Can there be a greater misery than to have God’s displeasure? What makes hell but the hiding of God’s face? And what makes God hide his face but sin? John 20:13: “they have taken my Lord away, and I do not know where they have put Him.” So our sins have taken away the Lord, and we do not know where he is laid. The favor of God is the best jewel: it can sweeten a prison and take the sting out of death. Oh how hateful then is the sin that robs us of our best jewel! Sin made God desert his temple (Ezek 8:6). Sin makes him appear as an enemy and dress himself in armor. This makes the soul pursue sin with a holy malice and seek to be avenged on it. The deserted soul gives sin #emph[gall] and #emph[vinegar] to drink, and with the spear of mortification lets out its heart’s blood.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. Desertion works for good as it produces these three gracious effects.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. It sets the soul weeping for the loss of God. When the sun is gone, the dew falls; and when God is gone, tears drop from the eyes. How troubled #emph[Micah] was when he had lost his gods! Judg 18:24: “You#super[+] took the gods I had made … What else do I have?” So when God is gone, what else do we have? It is not the harp and the viol that can comfort when God is gone. Though it is sad to lack God’s presence, yet it is good to lament his absence.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. Desertion sets the soul seeking after God. When Christ had stepped aside, the bride pursued him. She sought him “through the streets” of the city (Song 3:2). And when a #emph[non inventus] \[not found\] was returned, she raised a hue and cry after him (#emph[verse] 3): “Have you#super[+] seen the one I love?” The deserted soul sends up whole volleys of sighs and groans. It knocks at heaven’s gate by prayer; it can have no rest until the golden beams of God’s face shine.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. Desertion sets the Christian inquiring. He inquires into the cause of God’s departure: What is the accursed thing that has made God angry? Perhaps pride, perhaps a surfeit of ordinances, perhaps worldliness. Isa 57:17: “I was enraged by his sinful greed … and hid My face.” Perhaps there is some secret sin allowed. A stone in the pipe hinders the flow of water; so sin that is lived in hinders the sweet flow of God’s love. So conscience, like a bloodhound, finds out sin and overtakes it, and this #emph[Achan] is stoned to death.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[5\. Desertion works for good as it gives us a sight of what Jesus Christ suffered for us. If sipping the cup is so bitter, how bitter was the cup Christ drank on the cross? He drank a cup of deadly poison, which made him cry out, “My God, My God, why have You forsaken Me?” None can feel Christ’s sufferings so deeply, none can be so fired with love for Christ, as those who have been humbled by desertion and held over the flames of hell for a time.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[6\. Desertion works for good as it prepares the saints for future comfort. The nipping frosts prepare for spring flowers. It is God’s way first to cast down and then to comfort (2 Cor 7:6). When our Savior had been fasting, then the angels came and ministered to him. When the Lord has kept his people fasting long, then he sends the Comforter and feeds them with the hidden manna. Ps 97:11: אור זרע, “Light is sown for the righteous.” The saints’ comforts may be hidden like seed underground, but the seed is ripening, and will increase and flourish into a crop.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[7\. These desertions work for good as they will make heaven the sweeter to us. Here our comforts are like the moon: at times they are at the full, at times on the wane. God shows himself to us for a while, and then retires into the withdrawing room. How this will set off heaven the more, and make it more delightful and ravishing, when we will have a constant look of love from God (1 Thess 4:17)!]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[So we see that desertions work for good. The Lord brings us into the deep of desertion so that he may not bring us into the deep of damnation. He puts us into a seeming hell so that he may keep us from a real hell. God is fitting us for the time when we will enjoy his smiles forever, when there will be neither clouds on his face nor sunsetting, when Christ will come and stay with his bride and kiss her with the kisses of his lips, and the bride will never again say, “My beloved has withdrawn himself.”]]

=== SECTION IV. Showing that the evil of sin works for good to the godly.

4\. #smallcaps[The] evil of #emph[sin] works for good: not in its own nature (for it is damnable), but as God in his infinite wisdom overrules it. This is St. #emph[Augustine’s] comment on the text #footnote[#emph[Etiam peccatum ipsum in bonum convertit Deus.] \[God turns even sin itself to good.\] Augustine]. I may now say, as the apostle does in another sense, “Listen, I tell you#super[+] a mystery” (1 Cor 15:51): sin itself works for good to the godly. Indeed, it is a matter of wonder that any honey should come out of this lion. — We may understand it in a double sense.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. The sins of others work for good to the godly. It is no small trouble to a gracious heart to live among the wicked. Ps 120:5: “Woe to me that I dwell in Meshech.” Yet even this the Lord turns to good.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. The sins of others work for good as they breed holy sorrow. God’s people weep for what they cannot reform. Ps 119:136: “My eyes shed streams of tears because Your law is not obeyed.” #emph[David] was a mourner for the sins of the times; his heart was turned into a spring, and his eyes into rivers. Wicked men make merry with sin: “when you do evil, then you rejoice” (Jer 11:15). But the godly are #emph[weeping doves.] They grieve for the oaths and blasphemies of the age; they take the sins of others and make them spears to pierce their own souls. This grieving for the sins of others is good. 1. It shows a childlike heart. It is a mark of a noble nature to feel with sorrow the injuries done to our heavenly Father. 2. It shows a Christlike heart. Mark 3:5: He looked on them with “sorrow at their hardness of heart.” 3. The Lord takes special notice of these tears; he is well pleased that we should weep when his glory suffers. It shows more grace to grieve for the sins of others than for our own. We may grieve for our own sins out of fear of hell, but to grieve for the sins of others comes from a principle of love to God. These tears drop like water from the roses; they are sweet and fragrant, and God puts them in his bottle.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. The sins of others work for good to the godly as they set them praying the more against sin. If there were not such a spirit of wickedness abroad, perhaps there would not be such a spirit of prayer. #emph[Crying sins] cause #emph[crying prayers.] The people of God pray against the iniquity of the times, that God would put a check on sin and put sin to shame. If they cannot #emph[pray down] sin, they #emph[pray against] it; and God takes this kindly. These prayers will be both recorded and rewarded. Though we do not prevail in prayer, we will not lose our prayers. Ps 35:13: “my prayer returned to my own bosom.”]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. The sins of others work for good as they make us the more in love with grace. The sins of others are a foil to set off the luster of grace the more. One contrary sets off another: deformity sets off beauty. The sins of the wicked greatly disfigure them. Pride is a disfiguring sin: an ambitious man is only a bladder the devil has blown up. Now seeing another’s pride makes us the more in love with humility. Malice is a disfiguring sin; it is the devil’s picture. The more of this we see in others, the more we fall in love with meekness and charity. Drunkenness is a disfiguring sin. Like #emph[Circe’s] cup, it turns men into beasts; it robs them of the use of reason. The more intemperate we see others to be, the more we fall in love with sobriety. The black face of sin sets off the beauty of holiness so much the more.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. The sins of others work for good as they work in us a stronger opposition to sin. Ps 119:126: “they have broken Your law. Therefore I love Your commandments.” #emph[David] would never have loved God’s law so much if the wicked had not set themselves so much against it. The more #emph[violent] others are against the truth, the more #emph[valiant] the saints are for it. Living fish swim against the stream; the more the tide of sin comes in, the more the godly swim against it. The wickedness of the times stirs up holy passion in the saints: the anger that is against sin is without sin. The sins of others are like a whetstone to set a sharper edge on us; they whet our zeal and indignation against sin the more.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[5\. The sins of others work for good as they make us more earnest in working out our salvation. When we see wicked men take such pains for hell, this makes us more diligent for heaven #footnote[#emph[Impii quam strenue serviunt Diabolo!] \[How vigorously the wicked serve the devil!\] Cyprian.]. The wicked have nothing to encourage them, yet they sin. They risk shame and disgrace; they break through all opposition. Scripture is against them, and conscience is against them; there is a flaming sword in their way; yet they sin. Lam 5:9: “We get our bread at the risk of our lives.” Sinners eat the bread of wickedness at the risk of their souls. When godly hearts see the wicked so mad for the #emph[forbidden fruit,] wearing themselves out in the devil’s service, they are the more emboldened and quickened in the ways of God; they will take heaven, as it were, by storm. The wicked are #emph[swift dromedaries] in sin (Jer 2:23); and do we creep like snails in religion? Will impure sinners do the devil more service than we do Christ? Will they make more haste to a prison than we do to a kingdom? Are they never weary of sinning, and are we weary of praying? Do we not have a better master than they? Are not the paths of virtue pleasant? Is there not joy in the way of duty, and heaven at the end? The activity of the sons of #emph[Belial] in sin is a spur to the godly to make them mend their pace and run the faster to heaven.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[6\. The sins of others work for good as they are mirrors in which we may see our own hearts. Do we see a wicked, ungodly sinner? Behold a picture of our own hearts: so we would be if God left us. What is in other men’s #emph[practice] is in our #emph[nature.] Sin in the wicked is like fire on a beacon that flames and blazes out; sin in the godly is like fire in the embers. #emph[Christian,] though you do not break out into a flame of scandal, yet you have no cause to boast, for there is much sin raked up in the embers of your nature. You have #emph[the root of bitterness] in you, and you would bear as hellish fruit as anyone, if God did not either curb you by his power or change you by his grace.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[7\. The sins of others work for good as they are a means to make the people of God more thankful. When you#super[+] see another infected with the plague, how thankful you#super[+] are that God has preserved you#super[+] from it! It is a good use that may be made even of the sins of others, to be more thankful. Why might God not have left us to the same “flood of reckless indiscretion”? Think to yourself, O Christian: why should God be more gracious to you than to another? Why should he take you out of the wild olive of nature, and not him? How this may make you adore free grace! What the #emph[Pharisee] said boastingly, we may say thankfully. Luke 18:11: “God, I thank You that I am not like the other men—swindlers, evildoers, adulterers,” and so on. So we are to adore the riches of grace that we are not like others, drunkards, swearers, Sabbath-breakers. Every time we see men running riot in sin, we are to bless God that we are not such. If we see a madman, we bless God that it is not so with us. Much more, when we see others under the power of Satan, we are to give our thankful acknowledgment that it is not our condition.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[8\. The sins of others work for good as they are a means to make God’s people better. #emph[Christian,] God can make you a gainer by another’s sin: the more unholy others are, the more holy you are. The roses that grow near garlic are the sweetest; so another’s foulness makes a Christian’s graces send out a more fragrant perfume. The more a wicked man gives himself to sin, the more a godly man gives himself to prayer. Ps 109:4: “but I give myself to prayer.”]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[9\. The sins of others work for good as they give us occasion to do good. If there were no sinners, we could not be in such a position for service. The godly are often a means to convert the wicked: their prudent advice and godly example is a #emph[lure] and bait to draw sinners to embrace the gospel. The disease of the patient works for the good of the physician: by emptying the patient of harmful, diseased humors, the physician enriches himself. So by turning sinners from #emph[the error of their way,] our crown comes to be enlarged. Dan 12:3: “those who lead many to righteousness will shine like the stars forever and ever.” Not like lamps or tapers, but #emph[sicut stellae,] like the #emph[stars,] forever. So we see the sins of others work for our good.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. Our #emph[own sins] will work for good. This must be understood with care. When I say that the sins of the godly work for good, I do not mean that there is the least good in sin. Sin is like poison, which corrupts the blood, infects the heart, and without a sovereign antidote brings death. Such is the venomous nature of sin: it is deadly and damning #footnote[#emph[Peccato rum oleum spinas Gehennales facit pullulare] \[The oil of sins makes the thorns of hell spring up.\] Born.]. Sin is worse than hell. Yet God, by his mighty overruling power, makes sin in the outcome turn to the good of his people. God can make an antidote out of this poison. That is why St. #emph[Augustine] has that golden saying, “God would never permit evil if he could not bring good out of evil.” The relics and remains of corruption in the saints work for good in several ways.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. Sin makes them weary of this life. That sin is in the godly #emph[is sad,] but that it is a burden to them #emph[is good.] St. #emph[Paul]’s afflictions (pardon the expression) were only a game to him compared with his sin. He rejoiced in tribulation (2 Cor 7:4). But how this bird of paradise wept and bemoaned himself under his sins! Rom 7:24: “Who will rescue me from this body of death?” A believer carries his sins as a prisoner carries his shackles. Oh how he longs for a jail delivery! This feeling of sin is good.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. This indwelling of corruption makes the saints prize Christ more. He who feels his sin as a sick man feels his sickness, how welcome Christ the physician is to him! He who feels himself stung with sin, how precious the bronze serpent is to him! When #emph[Paul] had cried out about a #emph[body of death,] how thankful he was for Christ! Rom 7:25: “Thanks be to God, through Jesus Christ our Lord!” Christ’s blood saves from sin; it is the sacred ointment that kills this quicksilver.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. Sin works for good as it is an occasion of setting the soul on six excellent duties.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. It sets the soul on searching itself. A child of God, being conscious of sin in himself, takes the candle and lantern of the word and searches into his heart. He wants to know the worst of himself, as a man who is sick in body wants to know the worst of his disease. Though our joy lies in the knowledge of our graces, yet there is some benefit in the knowledge of our corruptions. That is why #emph[Job] prays, “Reveal to me my transgression” (Job 13:23). It is good to know our sins, so that we may not flatter ourselves or take our condition to be better than it is. It is good to find out our sins, before they find us out.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. The sin that remains in him sets a child of God on abasing himself. Sin is left in a godly man like a cancer in the breast or a hump on the back, to keep him from being proud. Gravel and dung are good to ballast a ship and keep it from overturning; the dung of sin helps to ballast the soul, so that it is not overturned with vainglory. We read of the #emph[spots of God’s children] (Deut 32:5). When a godly man looks at his face in the mirror of Scripture and sees the spots of unbelief and hypocrisy, this makes the plumes of pride fall; they are humbling spots. It is a good use that may be made even of our sins, when they cause low thoughts of ourselves. Better is the sin that humbles me#footnote[#emph[Melius est peccatum humilians, quam justitia inflans.] \[Better is the sin that humbles than the righteousness that puffs up.\] Augustine.] than the duty that makes me proud. Holy #emph[Bradford] said these words of himself: “I am,” he says, “a painted hypocrite.” And #emph[Hooper] said, “Lord, I am hell, and you are heaven.” From the thorn of sin the saints have gathered the grape of humility.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. Sin sets a child of God on judging himself. He is #emph[sui judex]\; he passes sentence on himself. Prov 30:2: “I am more brutish than any man.” It is dangerous to judge others, but it is good to judge ourselves. 1 Cor 11:31: “if we judged ourselves properly, we would not come under judgment.” When a man has judged himself, Satan is put out of office. When Satan lays anything to a saint’s charge, the saint is able to answer back and say, “It is true, Satan, I am guilty of these sins, but I have already judged myself for them. And since I have condemned myself in the lower court of conscience, God will acquit me in the upper court of heaven.”]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. Sin sets a child of God on conflict with himself. The spiritual self is in conflict with the carnal self. Gal 5:17: “the Spirit craves what is contrary to the flesh.” Our life is a wayfaring life and a warfaring life; there is a duel fought every day between the #emph[two seeds.] A believer will not let sin have peaceable possession. If he cannot keep sin #emph[out,] he will keep sin #emph[under.] Though he cannot quite overcome, yet he is overcoming (Rev 2:7): τῶ νικῶντι, #emph[to him who is overcoming.]]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[5\. Sin sets a child of God on watching himself. He knows sin is a traitor in his bosom, so he watches himself narrowly. A subtle heart needs a watchful eye. The heart is like a castle that is in danger of being assaulted every hour. This makes a child of God always stand sentry and keep a guard about his heart. A believer keeps a strict eye on himself, so that he may not fall into any scandalous enormity and so open a sluice to let all his comfort run out.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[6\. Sin sets the soul on reforming itself#footnote[#emph[Vt ignes ignibus extinguuntur, venenis venena depelluntur, calores Febrium medicinalibus caloribus franguntur, ita nihil impedit quin peccata peccatis curentur.] \[As fires are put out by fires, poisons are driven out by poisons, and the heats of fevers are broken by medicinal heats, so nothing hinders sins from being cured by sins.\] Augustine #emph[contra] Pelag.]. A child of God does not only find out sin, but drives out sin. One foot he sets on the neck of his sins, and the other foot he #emph[turns to God’s testimonies] (Ps 119:59). So the sins of the godly work for good: God makes the saints’ #emph[maladies] their #emph[medicines.]]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But let no one abuse this doctrine. I do not say that sin works for good to an impenitent person; no, it works for his damnation. It is #emph[to those who love God.] And as for you#super[+] who are godly, I know you#super[+] will not draw a wrong conclusion from this, either to make light of sin or to make bold with sin #footnote[#emph[Ne dicas ero sine metu, etc.] \[Do not say, I will be without fear, etc.\] Luther.]. If you#super[+] did so, God would make it cost you#super[+] dearly. Remember #emph[David.] He ventured presumptuously on sin, and what did he get? He lost his peace; he felt the terrors of the Almighty in his soul. Though he had every help to cheerfulness (he was a king, he was of a ruddy, sanguine complexion, he had skill in music), yet nothing could bring him comfort. He complains of his #emph[broken bones] #footnote[#emph[Longo tempore cum aculeo & morsu illius peccati conflictatur, quantumvis remissum.] \[For a long time he struggles with the sting and bite of that sin, however fully it has been forgiven.\] Luther on #emph[Genesis.]] (Ps 51:8). And though he did at last come out of that dark cloud, yet some divines are of the opinion that he never recovered his full joy to his dying day. If any of God’s people should tamper with sin because God can turn it to good, though the Lord does not damn them, he may send them to hell in this life. He may put them into such bitter agonies and convulsions of soul as may fill them with horror and bring them near to despair. Let this be a flaming sword to keep them from coming near the #emph[forbidden tree.]]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And so I have shown that both the best things and the worst things, by the overruling hand of the great God, work together for the good of the saints.]]
